        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/global-certifications/leather-working-group-lwg',
        'title' => 'LWG Certification India: Audit, Traceability & Scoring',
        'description' => 'LWG (Leather Working Group) certification for tanneries and leather brands in India: audit protocol, scoring, traceability, audit prep and cost.',
        'lead' => 'LWG full form is Leather Working Group, the global environmental audit standard for leather manufacturers. Tanneries and leather processors in India use an LWG rating to supply brands that only buy certified leather.',
        'sections' => [
            ['What is LWG certification?', '<p>LWG certification is the result of an audit against the <strong>LWG Leather Manufacturer Standard</strong>, run by the Leather Working Group, a not-for-profit body of brands, tanneries, chemical suppliers and others. The current version of the standard covers 17 sections, from operating permits, energy, water and effluent treatment to chemical management, restricted substances, traceability and social audit.</p><p>Brands in footwear, apparel, furniture and automotive interiors use the rating to choose suppliers, so Indian tanneries in Kanpur, Chennai, Ambur, Kolkata and Jalandhar that export to them are usually asked for it. See also our guide to <a href="/resources/blogs/start-a-profitable-leather-belt-manufacturing-business">starting a leather business in India</a> and <a href="/services/testing/textiles-leather/leather-footwear-testing">leather and footwear testing</a>.</p>'],
            ['LWG audit scoring: Gold, Silver, Bronze and Audited', '<p>Each scored section gets a percentage. The thresholds are:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Rating</th><th>Minimum score in each critical section</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Gold</td><td>85%</td></tr><tr><td>Silver</td><td>75%</td></tr><tr><td>Bronze</td><td>65%</td></tr><tr><td>Audited</td><td>50%</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>The final rating is the <strong>lowest</strong> rating achieved in any critical section, so a tannery that scores Gold in most sections but Silver in effluent treatment is rated Silver. Some questions are threshold questions that cap the rating regardless of the section score. A facility below the Audited threshold does not pass.</p>'],
            ['LWG traceability requirements', '<p>The audit checks how much of your incoming hides and skins can be traced back to their origin, and how. Traceability is scored separately from the medal rating at present, but LWG has said it will become a critical section in a future version of the standard.</p><ul>
<li>Physically marked to a slaughterhouse: weighted 100%.</li>
<li>Documented to a slaughterhouse: 80%.</li>
<li>Traced to a group of slaughterhouses: 70%.</li>
<li>Traced to a region or collection point: 60%.</li>
</ul><p>Tanneries buying from many small collectors usually start with regional traceability, then improve supplier records batch by batch.</p>'],
            ['LWG subcontractor audit', '<p>Units that carry out part of the process for a certified leather manufacturer, for example finishing or splitting on job work, are audited under LWG’s subcontractor route. A subcontractor that passes is listed as <strong>Audited</strong>; medal ratings are reserved for leather manufacturers. If your buyer’s certified tannery sends work to you, it may need your audit to keep its own rating.</p>'],
            ['LWG audit preparation: the steps we follow', '<ol>
<li>Confirm the right protocol: leather manufacturer, commissioning manufacturer, subcontractor or trader.</li>
<li>Gap assessment against every section, using your last 12 months of data.</li>
<li>Close the gaps: permits, ETP records, chemical inventory and restricted substances list testing, energy and water metering, traceability records.</li>
<li>Collect the production and environmental data the auditor will ask for.</li>
<li>Mock audit and corrections.</li>
<li>Schedule the audit with an LWG-approved auditor and support you on the day.</li>
</ol>'],
            ['LWG timeline and cost', '<p>Most tanneries need two to six months to prepare, depending on their gaps, then a few weeks for the audit and the report. The cost has three parts: LWG membership, the audit fee charged by the LWG-approved audit body (including auditor travel), and whatever upgrades your gap assessment shows, such as metering or ETP improvements. We quote our own support separately after the gap assessment.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the full form of LWG?', 'LWG stands for Leather Working Group. It is a not-for-profit organisation that runs the most widely used environmental audit standard for leather manufacturers.'],
            ['How is the LWG audit scored?', 'Each section is scored as a percentage, and the thresholds are 85% for Gold, 75% for Silver, 65% for Bronze and 50% for Audited. The overall rating is the lowest one achieved across the critical sections.'],
            ['What is LWG traceability?', 'It is the share of your raw material that can be traced back to a slaughterhouse, a group of slaughterhouses or a region of collection. Physical marking scores highest, followed by documented traceability.'],
            ['How long is LWG certification valid?', 'Leather manufacturer certification is valid for two years. A re-audit in the two months before expiry continues the certificate from the old expiry date, without a gap.'],
        ],
    ],

