        'description' => 'ICEGATE registration explained: who needs it, fees, documents, step-by-step online process, authorisation letter format and port (AD code) registration.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>ICEGATE registration is free.</strong> An importer or exporter needs an IEC, PAN, a Class 3 digital signature certificate (DSC), and an email ID and mobile number for verification. Once approved, you can file, pay and track customs documents online.</p><p lang="hi">आइसगेट पंजीकरण क्या है? यह भारतीय सीमा शुल्क के ऑनलाइन पोर्टल ICEGATE पर आयातकों और निर्यातकों का निःशुल्क पंजीकरण है, जिससे वे सीमा शुल्क दस्तावेज़ ऑनलाइन दाखिल और ट्रैक कर सकते हैं।</p>',
        'sections' => [
            ['ICEGATE authorisation letter format', '<p>Where a person other than the proprietor, partner or director registers or signs on behalf of the business, keep an authorisation letter on the company’s letterhead. A simple format:</p><blockquote><p>Date: ____<br>To: The ICEGATE Helpdesk / Commissioner of Customs</p><p>Subject: Authorisation for ICEGATE registration – IEC ____</p><p>We, M/s ____ (IEC ____, PAN ____), authorise Mr/Ms ____, ____ (designation), to register on ICEGATE and to file and sign customs documents on our behalf using their Class 3 digital signature certificate. Their specimen signature is given below.</p><p>Specimen signature of authorised person: ____</p><p>For M/s ____<br>Signature, name and designation of proprietor/partner/director, with company seal</p></blockquote><p>Upload it with the other documents when the portal asks for proof of authorisation.</p>'],
            ['Port registration and AD code: fees and steps', '<p>Exporters also register their bank’s 14-digit <strong>Authorised Dealer (AD) code</strong> with customs, so export proceeds and incentives are linked to the right bank account. There is no customs fee; your bank may charge for issuing the AD code letter.</p><ol>
<li>Ask your bank for the AD code letter on its letterhead.</li>
<li>Log in to ICEGATE with your DSC.</li>
<li>Upload the AD code letter and a cancelled cheque through e-Sanchit and submit the AD code registration.</li>
<li>Customs verifies and approves it, usually within a working day. One registration now covers all customs ports.</li>
</ol><p>Read our full guide to <a href="/resources/blogs/port-registration-ad-code-icegate">port registration and the AD code</a>, or let us handle <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/icegate-registration">ICEGATE registration</a> and your <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/import-export-code-iec">IEC</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['Is ICEGATE registration compulsory for export?', 'Not for every exporter, because a licensed customs broker can file shipping bills on your behalf. You do need it to file or track documents yourself, to register your AD code and to use most of the online customs services.'],
            ['What are port registration charges?', 'Customs charges no fee for AD code (port) registration on ICEGATE. Your bank may charge a small amount to issue the AD code letter.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'how-to-get-peso-certificate',
        'title' => 'PESO Full Form & How to Get a PESO Certificate (2026)',
        'description' => 'PESO full form is Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation. Licence types, documents, fees, timeline and how to download your PESO certificate online.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>PESO full form: Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation</strong> (earlier the Department of Explosives), under the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT), Ministry of Commerce and Industry. It is headed by the Chief Controller of Explosives in Nagpur and licenses petroleum storage, gas cylinders, pressure vessels and explosives. The application steps follow below.</p>',
        'sections' => [
            ['PESO licence types', '<table><thead><tr><th>Rules</th><th>What PESO licenses or approves</th><th>Typical applicants</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Petroleum Rules, 2002</td><td>Import, storage and transport of petroleum products</td><td>Fuel stations, storage depots, tankers, industries with diesel or furnace oil tanks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gas Cylinders Rules, 2016</td><td>Manufacture, import, filling and storage of gas cylinders, valves and regulators; cylinder testing stations</td><td>Cylinder importers, LPG and CNG filling plants, cylinder testing stations</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMPV (U) Rules, 2016</td><td>Static and mobile pressure vessels for compressed and liquefied gases</td><td>LPG, ammonia and industrial gas storage, road tankers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explosives Rules, 2008</td><td>Manufacture, possession, sale, transport and import of explosives</td><td>Mining, quarrying, fireworks and explosive manufacturers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Approvals under these rules</td><td>Flameproof and other electrical equipment for hazardous areas, and other equipment used with petroleum or gases</td><td>Makers and importers of Ex-rated motors, lights, panels and dispensers</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Calcium carbide and ammonium nitrate have their own rules, also administered by PESO.</p>'],
            ['How to download a PESO certificate online', '<p>PESO issues licences and approvals through its online portal (online.peso.gov.in), and they can be checked there:</p><ol>
<li>Licence holders log in to the portal with the user ID used for the application and download the signed licence from the application status page.</li>
<li>Anyone can check a licence or application from the portal’s public domain search: choose <strong>Status</strong>, select the rules the licence was issued under, and search by licence or application number.</li>
<li>For a vehicle’s CNG cylinder test certificate, choose Status, then <strong>Gas Cylinders Rules</strong>, then <strong>Search CNG Certificate by Vehicle Number</strong>. See our <a href="/resources/blogs/peso-online-cng-certificate">PESO CNG certificate download guide</a>.</li>
</ol><p>Menu names on the portal change from time to time; if an option has moved, use the portal’s help section or contact PESO.</p>'],
            ['PESO fees and timeline', '<p>Fees are set in the schedule of each set of rules and depend on the licence type, the quantity or capacity, and the number of years. They are paid online when you submit the application. Simple approvals can be granted in a few weeks; licences that need a site layout approval, inspection or product testing usually take two to three months. We confirm the fee and timeline for your case before filing; see <a href="/certification/india/peso-certification">PESO certification</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the full form of PESO?', 'PESO stands for Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation. It works under DPIIT, Ministry of Commerce and Industry, and is headed by the Chief Controller of Explosives.'],
            ['How do I download a PESO licence online?', 'Log in to online.peso.gov.in with the user ID used to apply and download the licence from your application status. To check someone else’s licence, use the public domain search by licence or application number.'],
            ['What is PESO approval?', 'PESO approval is permission from PESO to manufacture, import, store or use a regulated product or premises, such as a gas cylinder design, a petroleum storage tank or flameproof electrical equipment. It is issued under the relevant rules after the documents, drawings, tests or inspection are accepted.'],
            ['Is CCOE the same as PESO?', 'CCOE is the Chief Controller of Explosives, the head of PESO. A “CCOE approval” or “CCOE licence” is issued by PESO, so in practice the terms refer to the same authority.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/emea/g-mark-lved',
        'title' => 'G-Mark Certification: Benefits & Step-by-Step Process',
        'description' => 'Benefits of G-Mark certification for GCC markets and the full process: product scope, testing, technical file, notified body review, timeline and cost.',
        'headline' => 'G-Mark Certification for Low Voltage Equipment: Benefits and Process',
        'lead' => 'G-Mark lets you sell low-voltage electrical products in all seven GCC countries – Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain, Oman and Yemen – with one approval under the Gulf technical regulation BD-142004-01.',
        'sections' => [
            ['Benefits of G-Mark certification', '<ul>
<li><strong>One approval for seven markets.</strong> A single certificate is accepted in every GCC state, instead of separate national approvals.</li>
<li><strong>Smoother customs clearance.</strong> Customs can verify the product through the GCC Conformity Tracking Symbol (the QR code) at the port.</li>
<li><strong>Accepted by importers and retailers.</strong> Distributors, retail chains and marketplaces in the Gulf ask for the G-Mark before listing electrical goods.</li>
<li><strong>Protection against recalls and penalties.</strong> Market surveillance authorities can withdraw unmarked products and fine the importer.</li>
<li><strong>Builds on tests you already have.</strong> IEC test reports and CB certificates can often be used, so less retesting is needed.</li>
<li><strong>Proof buyers can check.</strong> Anyone can scan the QR code to confirm the certificate is genuine and current.</li>
</ul><p>See also our <a href="/resources/blogs/g-mark-cerification-guide-electronics-appliances-gcc-conformity">G-Mark guide for electronics and appliances</a> and <a href="/certification/emea/g-mark-toys">G-Mark for toys</a>.</p>'],
            ['G-Mark vs SASO and SABER', '<p>The G-Mark shows that a product meets the GCC-wide regulation for low-voltage equipment. <strong>SASO</strong> is Saudi Arabia’s standards body, and <strong>SABER</strong> is its online platform for product and shipment certificates under Saudi technical regulations. Exporters of electrical goods to Saudi Arabia usually deal with both: the G-Mark for the Gulf regulation and SABER for the Saudi registration and shipment certificate. We confirm which applies to your HS code; see <a href="/certification/emea/saber-certification">SABER certification</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['Which products need G-Mark?', 'Low-voltage electrical equipment rated between 50 and 1,000 V AC or 75 and 1,500 V DC that falls under the Gulf regulation BD-142004-01, such as household appliances, chargers and adapters, extension cords and many IT and AV products. The regulation lists the products and their standards.'],
            ['How long is G-Mark valid?', 'The Gulf Type Examination Certificate behind the G-Mark is generally valid for three years. Start the renewal before expiry, and reassess the product if the standards or the design change.'],
            ['Is G-Mark the same as SASO?', 'No. The G-Mark is the GCC conformity mark issued under Gulf technical regulations. SASO is Saudi Arabia’s standards organisation, whose SABER platform issues Saudi product and shipment certificates.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'is-2347-explained-mandatory-isi-mark-rules-for-pressure-cookers-in-india',
        'title' => 'IS 2347:2017 Pressure Cooker Standard: ISI Rules Explained',
        'description' => 'IS 2347 for pressure cookers: scope, key tests (safety valve, gasket, pressure), ISI marking rules, BIS licence process and penalties for unmarked cookers.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>IS 2347:2017 is the Indian Standard for domestic pressure cookers, and every pressure cooker made or sold in India must carry the ISI mark under it.</strong></p>',
        'top' => [
            ['IS 2347 at a glance', '<table><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Standard</th><td>IS 2347:2017 – Domestic Pressure Cookers, Specification</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Scope</th><td>Pressure cookers for household cooking</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Scheme</th><td>BIS product certification (ISI mark), mandatory under the Domestic Pressure Cooker Quality Control Order, 2020, in force from 1 February 2021</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Key tests</th><td>Operating pressure, safety valve or fusible plug release, hydraulic pressure test, lid locking, handles, gasket fit, material and thickness</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Related standard</th><td>Rubber gaskets for pressure cookers need their own ISI mark under IS 7466</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Details for applicants are on the <a href="/resources/standards/mandatory-bis-standard/isi-mark-domestic-pressure-cooker">ISI mark for domestic pressure cookers</a> standard page.</p>'],
        ],
        'sections' => [
            ['Key tests under IS 2347', '<ul>
<li><strong>Operating pressure:</strong> the pressure regulator (weight) must release steam within the specified pressure range.</li>
<li><strong>Safety devices:</strong> the safety valve or fusible plug must release pressure if the regulator is blocked, before the pressure becomes dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Hydraulic pressure test:</strong> the body and lid must withstand a test pressure well above the working pressure without leaking or deforming permanently.</li>
<li><strong>Lid and locking:</strong> the cooker must not open while under pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Handles:</strong> strength, secure fixing and surface temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Material and dimensions:</strong> the grade of aluminium or stainless steel, wall thickness, capacity and marking.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['How to check the ISI mark on a pressure cooker', '<ol>
<li>Look for the ISI mark with <strong>IS 2347</strong> above it and a licence number (CM/L-) below it, embossed or permanently marked on the body or lid.</li>
<li>Open the <strong>BIS Care</strong> app and use “Verify licence details” to enter the CM/L number.</li>
<li>Check that the licence is valid and that the brand and product match the cooker you are buying.</li>
</ol><p>A missing or unverifiable licence number is a sign of a fake mark; report it through the BIS Care app.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['How can I check if a pressure cooker’s ISI mark is genuine?', 'Note the CM/L licence number printed below the ISI mark and enter it in the BIS Care app under “Verify licence details”. The licence should be valid and in the name of the brand on the cooker.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/asia/ccc',
        'title' => 'China CCC Certification: Products, Process & Cost (2026)',
        'description' => 'China Compulsory Certificate (CCC) for Indian exporters: product catalogue, testing, factory audit, timeline and cost, including CCC for paints and coatings.',
        'lead' => 'CCC (China Compulsory Certification) is mandatory for products in China’s CCC catalogue before they are imported into, sold in or used in China.',
        'sections' => [
            ['CCC for paints and coatings', '<p>Two kinds of coatings are in the CCC catalogue, under the rules for decoration and renovation products (CNCA-C21-01:2024):</p><ul>
<li><strong>Solvent-based wood coatings</strong> (catalogue code 2101) for indoor furniture and woodwork, such as polyurethane, nitrocellulose and alkyd coatings.</li>
<li><strong>Water-based interior wall coatings</strong> (code 2104).</li>
</ul><p>Both need third-party certification: type testing, a factory check and annual surveillance. From 1 June 2026, certification is to the new limits for hazardous substances in coatings, GB 30981.1-2025 (architectural coatings) and GB 30981.2-2025 (industrial coatings), which replaced GB 18582-2020 and GB 18581-2020. Holders of certificates under the old standards have to convert them.</p><p>Other paints, such as exterior, industrial or automotive coatings, are not in the CCC catalogue, but they still have to meet China’s national limits for hazardous substances.</p>'],
            ['Which products need CCC in 2026', '<p>The catalogue currently covers <strong>17 categories and 106 product types</strong> (CNCA summary, April 2026), including:</p><ul>
<li>household appliances, audio-video and IT equipment, lighting, and power tools</li>
<li>cables, switches, plugs and sockets, low-voltage switchgear, and small motors</li>
<li>vehicles and parts such as tyres, safety glass, seat belts, lamps and mirrors</li>
<li>toys, child car seats and some building materials</li>
<li>fire safety products, security products and telecom terminal equipment</li>
</ul><p>From January 2026, 16 products moved from self-declaration to full third-party certification, among them fuses, small motors, electric drills, angle grinders, arc welders, automotive safety glass, seat belts and vehicle lamps. Check the HS code and the catalogue description together before you ship.</p>'],
            ['Factory inspection in the CCC process', '<p>For most products, the certification body inspects the factory before the certificate is issued and then every year. The inspectors check that production is consistent with the tested sample: critical components and suppliers, incoming inspection, routine and confirmation tests, calibration, handling of non-conforming products, and use of the CCC mark. Factories with a good record may be inspected less often.</p>'],
            ['CCC timeline and cost', '<p>A typical project takes three to four months: one to two weeks for the application and documents, four to eight weeks for type testing at a designated lab in China, one to two weeks for the factory inspection, and one to two weeks for the decision. The cost is made up of the application and certification fees of the certification body, type testing at the designated lab, the inspectors’ fees and travel for the factory inspection, and annual surveillance. We quote all of them for your product before you start.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is CCC certification?', 'CCC, or China Compulsory Certification, is the mandatory safety and quality mark for products listed in China’s CCC catalogue. Listed products cannot be imported, sold or used in China without it.'],
            ['Which products need CCC?', 'Products in the CCC catalogue, which in 2026 covers 17 categories and 106 product types, including household appliances, IT and AV equipment, lighting, cables, vehicle parts, toys and some building materials. The HS code alone is not enough: the catalogue description decides.'],
            ['Do paints need CCC certification?', 'Only two kinds do: solvent-based wood coatings and water-based interior wall coatings. Since 1 June 2026, both are certified to GB 30981.1-2025 or GB 30981.2-2025.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'how-to-get-isi-certification-for-mineral-water-plant',
        'title' => 'ISI Licence for Mineral Water Plant: Fees & IS 14543',
        'description' => 'Is an ISI licence still needed for a mineral water plant? What changed in 2024, FSSAI’s 2026 testing scheme, IS 14543 fees, lab and BIS steps.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>Since 17 October 2024, the ISI mark is no longer mandatory for packaged drinking water (IS 14543) or packaged natural mineral water (IS 13428).</strong> FSSAI removed the requirement, and from 1 January 2026 every plant must instead follow FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing. You still need an FSSAI licence to make and sell bottled water. The ISI licence is now voluntary, but many buyers and tenders still ask for it, and the steps below still apply if you choose to get one.</p>',
        'top' => [
            ['FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing for packaged water (from 1 January 2026)', '<p>Plants must test at an FSSAI-notified, NABL-accredited lab:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Source water:</strong> all parameters before use, and again whenever the source changes.</li>
<li><strong>Microbiological parameters:</strong> once a month, including coliforms, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and yeast and mould.</li>
<li><strong>Selected chemical parameters:</strong> once every three months, for example barium, copper, iron, nitrate and aluminium.</li>
<li><strong>Pesticide residues and all other parameters:</strong> once every six months.</li>
<li><strong>Packaging materials:</strong> bottles, jars, caps and pouches, once every six months.</li>
</ul><p>A failure increases the testing frequency, and repeated failures lead to a risk-based FSSAI inspection. Keep the records for at least five years.</p>'],
        ],
        'sections' => [
            ['ISI licence fees for packaged drinking water', '<p>For plants that choose the ISI mark, BIS charges:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Fee</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Application fee</td><td>₹1,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marking fee (IS 14543 and IS 13428)</td><td>₹20 per 1,000 litres for the first 6,000 kilolitres, ₹15 for the next 4,000, ₹10 thereafter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum annual marking fee</td><td>Lower for micro and small units under the BIS concessions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing and inspection</td><td>Charged at actuals by the BIS-recognised lab and for the factory inspection</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>GST applies on top. Confirm the current amounts on the BIS website before you apply; we include them at the published rate in your quote.</p>'],
            ['In-house lab and equipment list', '<p>An ISI licensee must be able to run the routine tests in the BIS scheme of testing and inspection, so BIS expects an in-house lab. Plants without ISI also keep one for daily checks on every batch. A typical setup has:</p><ul>
<li>a pH meter, conductivity and TDS meter, turbidity meter</li>
<li>an autoclave, an incubator and a laminar airflow bench for microbiology</li>
<li>a hot air oven, an analytical balance, a refrigerator and glassware</li>
<li>media and reagents for coliform, E. coli and yeast and mould tests</li>
</ul><p>The FSSAI-scheme tests listed above are done at an external notified lab, not in-house.</p>'],
            ['Licences a mineral water plant needs in 2026', '<ul>
<li>FSSAI licence (State or Central, depending on capacity and turnover)</li>
<li>Consent to establish and consent to operate from the State Pollution Control Board</li>
<li>Groundwater abstraction permission from the Central Ground Water Authority or the state authority</li>
<li>Factory licence and local trade licence</li>
<li>Plastic EPR registration as a brand owner</li>
<li>BIS ISI licence, now optional</li>
</ul>'],
            ['IS 14543 vs IS 13428', '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>IS 14543</th><th>IS 13428</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Product</td><td>Packaged drinking water (treated)</td><td>Packaged natural mineral water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Source</td><td>Any approved source, treated by RO, UV or ozonation</td><td>A protected underground source, bottled at source with minimal treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minerals</td><td>Can be added after treatment, within limits</td><td>Natural mineral content, must be stable</td></tr>
<tr><td>FSSAI regulation</td><td>2.10.8</td><td>2.10.7</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>See the <a href="/certification/india/bis-isi-mark-domestic-manufacturers">BIS ISI licence for domestic manufacturers</a> if you choose to certify.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the ISI licence fee for packaged drinking water?', 'BIS charges an application fee of ₹1,000, a marking fee based on volume (₹20 per 1,000 litres for the first 6,000 kilolitres, then lower), and testing and inspection charges at actuals. GST applies on top, and micro and small units get a concession on the minimum marking fee.'],
            ['Do I need FSSAI as well as ISI for a water plant?', 'FSSAI is required; ISI is not. Since October 2024, an FSSAI licence and compliance with FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing are what the law requires, and the ISI mark is voluntary.'],
        ],
        'faq_answers' => [
            'What is ISI certification for a mineral water plant?' => 'ISI certification is the Bureau of Indian Standards licence to use the ISI mark on packaged drinking water (IS 14543) or natural mineral water (IS 13428). It was mandatory until 17 October 2024 and is now voluntary; every plant still needs an FSSAI licence and must follow FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing.',
            'Is it mandatory to have an in-house testing laboratory?' => 'For an ISI licence, yes: BIS expects a lab that can carry out the routine tests in its scheme of testing and inspection. Without ISI, FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing requires periodic tests at an FSSAI-notified NABL lab, but most plants still keep an in-house lab for daily checks on each batch.',
        ],
        'replace' => [
            'making it mandatory for legal production and sale.' => 'which was mandatory for legal production and sale until FSSAI removed the requirement on 17 October 2024. It is now voluntary.',
            'ISI certification is mandatory for all entities producing or packaging natural mineral water for sale in India.' => 'Until 17 October 2024, ISI certification was mandatory for every unit producing or packaging natural mineral water or packaged drinking water for sale in India. It is now voluntary: plants need an FSSAI licence and must follow FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing, and those who choose the ISI mark follow the BIS steps below.',
            '<p>Yes, under the 2011 Regulations, no sales without it.</p>' => '<p>Not any more. FSSAI removed the mandatory BIS requirement on 17 October 2024, so an FSSAI licence and compliance with FSSAI’s Scheme of Testing (in force from 1 January 2026) are what you need to sell. The ISI mark is now voluntary.</p>',
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/emea/sfda-cosmetics',
        'title' => 'SFDA Cosmetics Registration in Saudi Arabia: Process & Cost',
        'description' => 'How to register cosmetics with the SFDA on the GHAD system (formerly eCosma): product listing, documents, labelling rules, timeline and cost.',
        'lead' => 'Cosmetics sold in Saudi Arabia must be listed (notified) with the SFDA before import. Listing is done on SFDA’s unified GHAD system, which replaced the older eCosma system in 2024.',
        'sections' => [
            ['Cosmetic labelling requirements in Saudi Arabia', '<ul>
<li>Product name and function, in Arabic, with English allowed alongside.</li>
<li>Full ingredient list using INCI names.</li>
<li>Net content by weight or volume.</li>
<li>Batch number, and the manufacturing and expiry dates, or the period after opening where it applies.</li>
<li>Name and address of the manufacturer and of the Saudi importer or listing holder, and the country of origin.</li>
<li>Directions for use and warnings, in Arabic.</li>
<li>Claims that match the evidence in your product file. SFDA can ask for a claim compliance certificate.</li>
</ul><p>The label you upload with the listing must be the one printed on the product.</p>'],
            ['Who needs SFDA cosmetic registration', '<p>Every cosmetic and personal care product placed on the Saudi market must be listed, including skin care, hair care, make-up, fragrances, oral care and toiletries. The listing is held by a company with a Saudi commercial registration, usually the importer or distributor, which needs a cosmetics account on GHAD. Indian manufacturers export through such a company and provide the documents. See our <a href="/resources/blogs/sfda-cosmetic-registration-saudi-arabia-ecosma-process-fees-timeline">SFDA cosmetic registration guide</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is eCosma?', 'eCosma was SFDA’s electronic system for notifying cosmetic products. In 2024 it was replaced by GHAD, SFDA’s unified electronic system, where cosmetic products are now listed under Product Services.'],
            ['How long does SFDA cosmetic registration take?', 'Plan for about two to five months in total: one to two weeks to set up the company account, three to eight weeks to compile the product file and any tests, and four to ten weeks for SFDA review. Queries from SFDA add time.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/man-power/bocw-registration',
        'title' => 'BOCW Registration: Full Form, Certificate & Fees',
        'description' => 'BOCW stands for Building and Other Construction Workers. Who must register, the 1% cess, fees, documents and what changed under the 2025 labour codes.',
        'lead' => 'BOCW full form is Building and Other Construction Workers. Employers carrying out building or construction work must register the establishment and pay the 1% construction cess; since 21 November 2025 this is done under the new labour codes, which replaced the two BOCW Acts of 1996.',
        'sections' => [
            ['What changed with the labour codes in 2025', '<p>Until November 2025, construction employers followed the <strong>Building and Other Construction Workers (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1996</strong> and the <strong>BOCW Welfare Cess Act, 1996</strong>. On 21 November 2025, the four labour codes came into force and replaced both:</p><ul>
<li>Registration of the establishment, safety and working conditions moved to the <strong>Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020</strong> (OSH Code).</li>
<li>The cess, the welfare boards and the registration of workers as beneficiaries moved to the <strong>Code on Social Security, 2020</strong>.</li>
</ul><p>Registrations already granted under the old Act continue, and states are moving their portals and rules to the codes. People still search for and refer to this as “BOCW registration”.</p>'],
            ['What is a BOCW certificate?', '<p>The term is used for two different documents:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Establishment registration certificate:</strong> issued by the state labour department’s registering officer to the employer for a construction project. It records the site, the nature of work, the expected number of workers and the duration.</li>
<li><strong>Worker’s registration (labour card):</strong> issued by the State Building and Other Construction Workers Welfare Board to a worker aged 18 to 60 who has done at least 90 days of construction work in the previous 12 months. It gives access to welfare schemes such as accident cover, education support and pensions.</li>
</ul><p>Both are issued online on the state’s labour portal, and the employer’s certificate can be downloaded from the portal once approved.</p>'],
            ['Who must register', '<p>An employer carrying out building or other construction work, such as buildings, roads, bridges, pipelines, towers and electrical lines, with 10 or more workers on any day must register the establishment, normally before work starts. Contractors and principal employers are both responsible for compliance on site. The construction of a small house for one’s own residence below the notified cost limit is generally excluded.</p>'],
            ['BOCW cess: the 1% fee on construction cost', '<p>A cess of <strong>1% of the cost of construction</strong> is payable by the employer. Since 8 May 2026, it is levied under section 100 of the Code on Social Security (notification S.O. 2322(E)), at the same rate as before. Employers now assess the cess themselves, with a final assessment after the project is completed; on government and public sector projects it is usually deducted from the contractor’s bills. Land cost and the compensation paid to workers are not part of the cost of construction. The money goes to the state welfare board.</p>'],
            ['BOCW registration fees', '<p>The establishment registration fee is set by each state’s rules and depends on the number of workers to be employed. It is paid online on the state labour portal along with the application. The 1% cess is separate from the registration fee.</p>'],
            ['Documents and process for BOCW registration', '<ol>
<li>Collect the project details: site address, nature of work, start and end dates, estimated cost and the maximum number of workers.</li>
<li>Keep ready the PAN and registration of the employer, the work order or contract, the approved building plan or project approval, and the ID of the authorised signatory.</li>
<li>Apply on your state’s labour portal and pay the registration fee.</li>
<li>Answer any query from the registering officer; the certificate is issued online.</li>
<li>Assess and pay the 1% cess, and file returns and notices as the state requires, including notice when the work is completed.</li>
</ol>'],
